{"amendments":[{"body":"Use the canonical concentration helpers in the core limitation code before merging. `crates/tank_core/src/systems/nitrogen_cycle.rs:76-90` still computes decomposer DO/DOC limitation from raw totals, `crates/tank_core/src/systems/algae_growth.rs:31-38` still uses raw ammonia/nitrate/phosphate totals for nutrient limitation, and `crates/tank_core/src/systems/plant_growth.rs:22-25,36-55` still uses raw DIC/N/P totals. Two tanks with identical concentrations but different water volumes will still get different biological rates, so the new helper layer is not yet the canonical source of truth.","rationale":"This is a correctness gap, not just cleanup: the branch introduces canonical concentration APIs specifically to stop volume-dependent total-mass kinetics from creeping back in, but the main biology systems still bypass them."},{"body":"Restore save compatibility instead of permanently rejecting every schema 2 file. `crates/tank_core/src/save.rs:8-49` now hard-rejects all schema 2 saves, even though the saved state plus the new helpers already provide the data needed to migrate them (`crates/tank_core/src/types/geometry.rs:19-21`, `crates/tank_core/src/types/state.rs:182-186`, `crates/tank_core/src/types/water.rs:179-202`). As written, upgrading to this build turns the units refactor into a load failure for every existing save.","rationale":"This is a user-facing compatibility regression. The refactor changes concentration semantics, but it should not strand previously saved simulations when a deterministic migration path is available."}],"summary":"Reviewed the branch diff against `origin/master`, read the concentration-helper implementation and key call sites end-to-end, and ran the full `cargo test` suite. I found two substantive issues: core biology still bypasses the new canonical concentration API, and save compatibility regressed by hard-rejecting schema 2 files."}